course: politics
lesson: 31
title: Propaganda and fake news
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Propaganda is a message made to push lots of people to think or act one way: it shows only the facts that help and leaves out the rest.
explain: Think of a cereal advert that shows the toy in the box and never the sugar; the old war poster "Your country needs YOU" did the same, with a pointing finger and no word about how dangerous war is.
explain: Fake news is something else: a made-up story dressed up to look like real news, with a copied logo and lots of likes, though likes only show that people clicked.
explain: Not everything wrong is a lie: a mistake fixed with a correction is honest, satire is a joke on purpose, and news you simply dislike is not "fake".
explain: The first check is quick: who wrote it, when, and does anyone else report it?
fact: Propaganda is a message made to push people towards one view, often by a government or a group, and it picks only the facts that help.
fact: In the First World War, posters such as "Your country needs YOU" were used to get men to join the army.
fact: When a government controls the TV, it can show only good news about the leader; public broadcasters such as the BBC, ABC or CBC are paid for by the public and meant to report freely.
fact: Fake news is a made-up story made to look like real news; logos are easy to copy, and likes show clicks, not truth.
fact: A mistake is getting it wrong by accident, a lie is saying what you know is false, and good outlets fix mistakes in the open with a correction.
fact: Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose.
fact: Satire is a joke that copies the style of news, like the made-up headlines on The Onion or The Beaverton.
fact: Propaganda often uses fear, anger, music and colours instead of reasons, and may call a whole group "enemies".
fact: The same event can be framed with loaded words, "brave citizens" or "an angry mob", so look for the plain facts both sides agree on.
fact: In the Second World War, posters saying "Careless talk costs lives" told people not to chat about soldiers or ships; people disagree whether safety messages like this count as propaganda.
fact: Calling a true story "fake news" because you dislike it does not make it made up, and it makes trust harder for everyone.
fact: A quote next to a famous face with no date and no link may be made up; a real quote can be found in a report, a video or a speech.
fact: Warning clues are no author, no date, a shouty headline, an odd web address and no other outlets; one clue is not proof, but several together are a warning.
fact: Words in this lesson: "no cap" means "no lie, for real"; "sus" means suspicious; "wee" is Scottish for small; "our kid" in Liverpool and Manchester means my brother or sister.
exercise: What is propaganda? | A message made to push people towards one view by showing only some facts | Any news story printed in a newspaper, whoever wrote it and whatever it says about anything | A joke that copies the style of the news | A mistake that a news outlet later corrects
exercise: Why were posters like "Your country needs YOU" made in the First World War? | To get men to join the army | To sell breakfast cereal to families | To tell people about the weather | To invite children to a school fair
exercise: What can happen when the government controls the only TV channel? | People may see only good news about the leader | People see every side of every story | The TV must criticise the leader every night, by law, to keep things fair | Nobody is allowed to watch any news at all
exercise: A post says schools will close on Fridays; it has a school logo and lots of likes. Is that proof? | No, logos are easy to copy and likes only show clicks | Yes, a school logo always means it is official and was sent by the head | Yes, if many people liked it, it must be true | Yes, posts with logos cannot be made up
exercise: The news gave the wrong day for a match and said sorry the next day. What was it? | A mistake that was fixed with a correction | A lie, because the news always lies | A piece of wartime propaganda | A joke from a satire website
exercise: What is the difference between misinformation and disinformation? | Misinformation is shared by mistake, disinformation on purpose | Misinformation is on TV, disinformation is in newspapers and on websites | Misinformation is true, disinformation is false | There is no difference, they mean the same thing
exercise: "Government to put a tax on sneezing" comes from a joke website. What is that called? | Satire | Propaganda | A correction | Breaking news
exercise: A video calls everyone who disagrees "enemies", with dramatic music and a red screen. What is it using? | Strong feelings instead of reasons | Careful facts gathered from many sources | A calm, fair look at both sides of it | A correction of an earlier mistake it made
exercise: One site says "thousands of brave citizens", another says "an angry mob". What is going on? | The same event is framed with different loaded words | One of the sites must have invented the protest from nothing, with fake photos | Both sites are joke websites | The two sites are about two different protests
exercise: What did "Careless talk costs lives" posters ask people to do? | Not to chat about soldiers or ships in case spies heard | To talk loudly so everyone knew the news | To buy more newspapers during the war | To stop writing letters to their families
exercise: A boy calls a storm report "fake news" because he dislikes the forecaster. Is it fake? | No, disliking a story does not make it made up | Yes, any story you dislike counts as fake news | Yes, weather reports are always made up by computers | Yes, because forecasters are not reporters
exercise: A shocking quote sits next to a leader's photo with no date and no link. What should you think? | It may be made up until you find where it was said | It must be real because the photo is real | It is real if it sounds like something they would say | It is real if it has a nice font
exercise: What three things does Mateo's dad tell him to check? | Who wrote it, when, and whether it has a name and a date | How many likes, how many shares and how many comments it has | The colours, the font and the size of the picture | Whether it is funny, scary or exciting
exercise: Which of these is a warning clue that a story might be fake? | No author, no date and no other outlets reporting it | A named reporter and a date at the top | The same story in several big news outlets | A link to the full report it describes
exercise: What does a good news outlet do when it gets something wrong? | It puts out a correction in the open | It deletes the story and says nothing | It blames the readers for the mistake | It says the mistake was a joke all along
exercise: Who pays for public broadcasters such as the BBC, ABC or CBC? | The public, and they are meant to report freely | Only the leader of the country, who controls them | Only joke websites like The Onion, out of their profits | Nobody, they are run by volunteers who work for free
test: "bro this Your Country Needs YOU poster is just an advert innit" — what was it really? | Propaganda to get men to join the army in the First World War | An advert for a new brand of breakfast cereal | A satire poster from a joke website | A correction printed by a newspaper
test: "no cap, school's shut every Friday from next month, it's got the logo and like 3k likes" — what is the best next step? | Check the school website or wait for a letter home | Share it with everyone so nobody misses it | Believe it, because 3k likes can't be wrong | Stay home on Friday just in case
test: "the news got the match day wrong, so they're all liars, end of" — what is the best answer? | A mistake fixed with a correction is not a lie | Yes, any mistake proves they lie on purpose | Yes, real news never makes any mistakes at all | Yes, so you should never watch news again
test: A Gen X uncle: "Whoa, rad, they're taxing sneezes now! Total bummer." The story is from The Onion. What happened? | He mistook satire for real news | He found a secret government plan | He read a correction about taxes | He saw some wartime propaganda on TV
test: Gran shared a fake flood story by accident, thinking it was true. Which word fits best? | Misinformation | Disinformation | Satire | Propaganda
test: In a Scottish village: "Och, this wee video says anyone who disagrees is the enemy, and the screen goes aw red. Pure convincing, aye?" What is the best reply? | It uses fear and colours instead of reasons, so slow down | Yes, red screens mean it is very important news | Yes, music only ever plays on true videos, never on the fake ones | Yes, calling people enemies proves it is right
test: What would happen if a country's only TV channel was run by the leader and never showed bad news? | People could not hear what the leader gets wrong | People would hear every side of every story | The leader would be criticised every night | The country would have no leader any more
test: In a Texas town: "Y'all, that storm report's fake news, I can't stand that weather guy." Which is true? | Disliking the forecaster does not make the report made up | He is right, weather reports are usually fake, made up in a studio | He is right, fake news means news you dislike | He is right, storms are never really reported
test: "this quote's well sus, no date no link, but it's got his face on so it's legit right?" What is the best reply? | Anyone can put words next to a photo; find where it was said | Yes, a real photo proves the words are real | Yes, a quote with a face next to it is always genuine and checked | Yes, if it sounds bad it must be true
test: In Liverpool: "Our kid says one site calls them brave citizens and the other an angry mob, so one of them made the whole protest up." What is more likely? | Both describe the same protest in different loaded words | One site invented a protest that never happened | Both sites are writing about two different cities | Neither site saw the protest, so it never happened at all
test: In a cartoon, a villain's poster shows only his smiling face and the words "He keeps you safe". Which real thing is that most like? | Propaganda that shows only the facts that help | A newspaper correction of an earlier mistake | A fair report giving both sides of a story | A weather report from a public broadcaster
test: "shut up, it's got 50k likes so it's true, obviously" — what is wrong with that? | Likes show how many people clicked, not whether it is true | Nothing, more likes always mean more truth | Only posts with 100k likes are true | Likes are counted by the government, so they prove it is true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises propaganda? | A communication designed to induce a particular view by the selective presentation of facts | Any account of events published by a newspaper or broadcaster | A humorous publication imitating the conventions of the news | An acknowledgement by a news outlet that an error has been made
exam: It is given that a news outlet published an incorrect date and subsequently apologised. Identify the term which most accurately describes its conduct. | The issuing of a correction in respect of an honest error | The deliberate dissemination of disinformation | The production of satire for the amusement of readers | The publication of state propaganda on behalf of the government
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the distinction between misinformation and disinformation. | The former is shared in error, whereas the latter is disseminated deliberately | The former is broadcast, whereas the latter is printed | The former is invariably true, whereas the latter is invariably false and malicious | The two terms are wholly synonymous and interchangeable
exam: Whereas a circulated item bears a copied logo and a great number of approvals but no author and no date, determine the conclusion most appropriate. | Its authenticity cannot be presumed and ought to be verified with the stated source | Its authenticity is established by the number of approvals received, provided it exceeds one thousand | Its authenticity is established by the presence of the logo | Its authenticity is established by the absence of any correction
